-The overall goal of the bipedal robot project is to create a high-fidelity biomimetic humanoid robot actuated by artificial muscles and controlled by a synthetic nervous system (SNS). This dissertation develops tools needed to pursue that goal. The work proceeds from characterizing actuator force and predicting joint torque to organizing sensory-afferent evidence and testing neural controllers in coupled mechanical models. The following sections explain why these four contributions are needed, place them within the robot project, state the research objectives, and describe the dissertation's organization.
+The overall goal of the bipedal robot project in the Agile and Adaptive Robotics Lab at Portland State University is to create a high-fidelity biomimetic humanoid robot actuated by artificial muscles and controlled by a synthetic nervous system (SNS). This dissertation develops tools needed to pursue that goal. The work starts with characterizing actuator force and predicting joint torque and proceeds to organizing sensory-afferent evidence and developing tools for constructing and testing neural controllers in coupled mechanical models. The following sections explain why these four contributions are needed, position them within the robot project, state the research objectives, and describe the dissertation's organization.
 
 \section{Motivation}
 
 The motivation for biomimetic robot design tools is grounded in two complementary halves of an iterative design loop. The first half draws on principles from biomechanics and neuroscience to develop robots with improved autonomy, balance, and adaptability in unstructured terrain \citep{frey_legged_2026, ha_learning_2025, ijspeert_amphibious_2020}. Compliant actuation and biologically inspired neural control also offer potential improvements in locomotor energy economy. The second half uses these robots as controllable physical models to investigate biological movement, including the mechanisms underlying motion generation, internal forces, and neural control \citep{asano_design_2017, hitzmann_anthropomorphic_2018}. Robotic platforms allow researchers to systematically manipulate actuator properties, mechanical configurations, and sensory pathways under repeatable conditions, including interventions that would be impractical or unethical in human participants. They therefore complement animal studies and numerical models by enabling the coupled mechanical and neural contributions to movement to be investigated experimentally \citep{shin_constructive_2018, goldsmith_investigating_2021}. This iterative process is illustrated by \citet{ijspeert_swimming_2007} using a salamander robot, in which biological evidence informed a spinal cord model, and subsequent robotic experiments generated predictions that were examined in biological preparations. This reciprocal approach also has potential applications in the development of prosthetic and orthotic devices. In these ways, biological understanding informs robotic design, while robotic experiments provide new hypotheses and insights that can be returned to biology. 
 
-The first mechanical design consideration is to select artificial muscles that can meet the robot joints' torque requirements within the available space. The McKibben-style braided pneumatic actuators (BPAs) used here combine low mass, high force-to-weight ratio, and intrinsic compliance with a force--length curve that qualitatively resembles that of skeletal muscle \citep{liang_comparative_2020, hunt_modeling_2017}. At shorter resting (uninflated) lengths, Festo BPAs delivered less force and contraction than what the manufacturer specifies. Existing empirical models describe useful pressure--contraction relationships \citep{sarosi_comparative_2017, martens_modeling_2017, hunt_modeling_2017}, but choosing actuator lengths required evaluating how resting length affects their output. It was therefore necessary to build a jig to perform isometric force experiments with Festo BPAs. This allows the development of force models that account for resting length, pressure, and contraction. The work in Chapter~\ref{ch:muscles} provides a basis for actuator sizing within the tested isometric conditions.
+The first mechanical design consideration is to select artificial muscles that can meet the robot joints' torque requirements within the available space. The McKibben-style braided pneumatic actuators (BPAs) used here combine low mass, high force-to-weight ratio, and intrinsic compliance with a force--length curve that qualitatively resembles that of skeletal muscle \citep{liang_comparative_2020, hunt_modeling_2017}. Our initial work with Festo BPAs showed that at shorter resting (uninflated) lengths, they delivered less force and contraction than what the manufacturer specifies. Existing empirical models describe useful pressure--contraction relationships \citep{sarosi_comparative_2017, martens_modeling_2017, hunt_modeling_2017}, but choosing actuator lengths required evaluating how resting length affects their output. Therefore, I built a jig to perform isometric force experiments with Festo BPAs. This allowed the development of force models that account for resting length, pressure, and contraction. The work in Chapter~\ref{ch:muscles} provides a basis for actuator sizing within the tested isometric conditions.
 
-The next mechanical design consideration is predicting how actuator force becomes joint torque over the required knee motion. A robot knee transmits actuator force to the skeleton through mounting brackets and artificial tendons. Actuator paths can bend the actuator as it crosses a joint. Here, musculotendon routes are the combined BPA and artificial-tendon paths through attachment and wrapping points. Brackets and fixtures can deflect under load, tendons can stretch, and contact with the knee bends an actuator and changes its usable length. Differences between nominal and effective musculotendon length, series compliance, and actuator bending can therefore cause rigid-body predictions to differ from measured torque. Experience showed that joint torque and range of motion (RoM) would be less than expected. Therefore, it was necessary to determine correction factors for more accurate joint prediction calculations. An isometric joint torque test jig was created. By testing isometric knee torque, and going from simple to complex configurations, it would allow for the  development of corrections to the prediction model. The improved model can be used to design routes that can meet selected human-muscle torque targets (Chapter~\ref{ch:torque}).
+The next mechanical design consideration is predicting joint torque of an assembled robot over the required knee motion. A robot knee transmits actuator force to the skeleton through mounting brackets and artificial tendons. Brackets and fixtures can deflect under load, tendons can stretch, and contact with the knee bends an actuator and changes its usable length. Differences between nominal and effective musculotendon length, series compliance, and actuator bending can therefore cause rigid-body predictions to differ from measured torque. Initial tests showed that joint torque and the usable range of motion (RoM) could be lower than predicted. I therefore built an isometric knee-torque test jig to develop correction factors, progressing from pinned-knee configurations to the biomimetic four-bar knee. The improved model was then used to design and test musculotendon routes against selected human-muscle torque targets (Chapter~\ref{ch:torque}).
 
-The first neural control design consideration is selecting sensory feedback pathways and defining how they influence a synthetic nervous system. Neural control architectures are often presented as schematic diagrams resembling flowcharts. These diagrams simplify biological neural circuits by showing selected connections and functional relationships, while implementing those relationships requires information about the pathways, their effects, and the conditions under which they operate. Therefore, it was necessary to gather and curate documented sensory pathways and rules describing how sensory input influences neural activity and movement, relying on computational models, animal experiments, and literature reviews. These findings are distributed across species, preparations, and experimental protocols, so their interpretation requires preserving the context in which they were reported \citep{van_de_crommert_review_1998, windhorst_muscle_2007, windhorst_spinal_2022}. The Sensory Afferent Database (SADb) was developed to organize this information into a searchable resource linked to its sources. The work in Chapter~\ref{ch:database} provides a database to select feedback pathways and define biologically informed interactions in neuromechanical models.
