-The next neural control design consideration is developing tools that enable synthetic neural controllers to be constructed, coupled to different mechanical models, and tested efficiently. Earlier controller-transfer work with AnimatLab required substantial manual adaptation and parameter tuning using software not updated since 2016. This motivated a more flexible workflow for combining neural and mechanical models. Published neural architectures and simulation software provide a foundation for this work \citep{rybak_modelling_2006, mccrea_organization_2008, markin_afferent_2010, nourse_sns_2023}. Constructing a coupled model also requires interfaces that connect neural outputs to actuation and mechanical states to sensory feedback. Graphical, block-based programming supports the construction and inspection of neural circuits and their signal paths, while headless simulation supports repeated experiments and automated parameter tuning without continuous interaction with a graphical interface. It was therefore necessary to develop tools that support both interactive model construction and programmatic simulation, with automated tuning to reduce reliance on manual parameter adjustment. The work in Chapter~\ref{ch:sns} brings graphical neural-modeling tools, simulation interfaces, and automated scripts together to support building and testing synthetic neural controllers with human biomechanical models and BPA-actuated robot models.
